 "stale": false,
 "narrative": "No regions are included in Trade prices, all trades are aggregated across the state during the reporting period. Price Unit marked as Per Bundle are for 21 small bale bundles. Quality Grades based on visual appearance and intent of sale (useage) where test data was unavailable.  Good demand was seen for hay across the state, with active buyer activity purchasing hay, with trades being noted both to the southeast and moving west.  Dry pasture conditions over the last month have helped with harvest and sale efforts, but has reduced the yield coming off later-cuts of hay.  According to the most recent NASS Crop Progress Report, Tennessee's Pasture Condition rates 26% Poor, 31% Fair, and 20% Good.  \n\nAccording to the National Integrated Drought Information System, currently 64.2% of Tennessee falls within any Drought & Dryness Category (D1-D4), with 21.1% falling into D0 (Abnormally Dry) and 16.1% falling into D1 (Moderate Drought) focused in Middle and Western TN.  29.4% falls within D2 (Severe Drought) and 17.6% within D3 (Extreme Drought), mostly focused in Western and Middle TN, with a small portion of D4 (Extreme Drought) seen in Western TN.  See more information at; https://www.drought.gov/states/tennessee\n\nAlfalfa Quality Guidelines \nQuality         ADF      NDF      *RFV/RFQ     **TDN-100%    **TDN-90%      CP \nSupreme   <27         <34        >185               >62                   >55.9                >22\nPremium    27-29     34-36   170-185         60.5-62            54.5-55.9          20-22\nGood           29-32     36-40   150-170         58-60               52.5-54.5         18-20\nFair              32-35     40-44   130-150         56-58                50.5-52.5        16-18\nUtility          >35         >44      <130              <56                    <50.5                <16\n \n*RFV calculated using the Wis/Minn formula. **TDN calculated using the Western formula. Quantitative factors are approximate, and many factors can affect feeding value. Values based on 100% dry matter (TDN showing both 100% & 90%). Guidelines are to be used with visual appearance and intent of sale (usage).\n\nGrass Hay guidelines\nQuality                Crude Protein Percent\nPremium            Over 13\nGood                    9-13\nFair                       5-9\nUtility                   Under 5",
 "source": "USDA AMS Market News",
 "source_url": "https://mymarketnews.ams.usda.gov/viewReport/3793",
